-- title:
--   Type-ψ additive characters modulo Pi₀ span at most a line
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ and an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$, together with a ring homomorphism $j_0$ from $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ (the Witt vectors of $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$) to $k$, and let $X_0$ be a special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $k$ for $j_0$: a commutative two-dimensional formal group law $F$ over $k$ carrying an action $a \mapsto \mathrm{act}\,a$ of $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ by endomorphisms and an endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = [q]$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}\,a = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$, such that the two eigenspaces $\mathrm{lieZero} = \bigcap_a \ker(\mathrm{lieAct}\,a - j_0(a))$ and $\mathrm{lieOne} = \bigcap_a \ker(\mathrm{lieAct}\,a - j_0(\sigma a))$ of $\mathrm{Lie}\,X_0$ are complementary and invertible, and such that $[q]$ has kernel of degree $q^4$. The hypothesis `hsmooth` asserts that the linear part of $\varpi$ does not annihilate both of these eigenspaces. Let $\psi : \mathbb{Z}_{q^2} \to k$ be any function and let $g, g' \in k[[X_0,X_1]]$ have zero constant term. Assume that the additive coboundaries $g(F(X,Y)) - g(X) - g(Y)$ and likewise for $g'$ lie in the ideal generated by the components of $\varpi(X)$ and of $\varpi(Y)$ in $k[[X_0,X_1,Y_0,Y_1]]$, and that for every $a \in \mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ both $g \circ \mathrm{act}\,a - \psi(a)\,g$ and $g' \circ \mathrm{act}\,a - \psi(a)\,g'$ lie in the ideal generated by the components of $\varpi$. Then there exist $c, c' \in k$, not both zero, with $c\,g + c'\,g'$ in the ideal generated by the components of $\varpi$.
--
--   The statement bounds by $1$ the dimension of the space of additive characters of the finite group scheme $X_0[\Pi]$ of a fixed $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$-type $\psi$, at a point of the moduli problem where the linear part of $\Pi$ survives on one of the two eigenlines of the Lie algebra; the hypothesis `hsmooth` is exactly the exclusion of the double points. It is obtained from the corresponding statement in the normal form $(\,X_1, X_0^{q^2}\,)$ for the ideal generated by $\varpi$, via the coordinate change provided by `exists_subst_eq_X_and_span_subst_varpi_eq_of_not_and` and `exists_conj_of_subst_eq_X`, and is used in turn in the analysis of $\Pi$-divisible coboundaries of a given type in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_SpecialFormalODModule_exists_smul_add_smul_mem_span_varpi_of_addCoboundary_mem_of_not_and.lean

